F15B                             ; ---- machine code: the grave -----------------------------------------------
F15B                             ; "`" plus a hex digit loads bytes: ( addr -- addr' ). Pairs of hex digits
F15B                             ; are stored from addr until a non-hex character; then a RTS (39h) is
F15B                             ; written and its address is left. An odd last digit is consumed and dropped.
F15B                             ; "`" plus anything else calls the machine code at TOS: ( addr -- ). The
F15B                             ; code is entered by JSR (the dispatcher's own), with the address already
F15B                             ; popped: U points at the top data cell (big-endian, 2 bytes, stack grows
F15B                             ; down) and the return address is on top of S. The code may pop and push
F15B                             ; data cells by changing U; on return the cell at ,U is the new TOS. It must
F15B                             ; keep Y (the interpreter's reading position), DP and S (balanced), and
F15B                             ; return with RTS. A, B, X, CC may change.
F15B                             ; Neither the loader's stores nor the code are checked, in either build.
F15B   8D 20        H_GRAVE:   BSR   hexc   ; the digit after the grave, if any
F15D   24 1A                  BCC   exec   
F15F   AE C4                  LDX   ,U   
F161   C6 10        HX1:      LDB   #16   
F163   3D                     MUL      ; B = the digit, moved up to the high half
F164   E7 84                  STB   ,X   ; (an odd digit is overwritten by the RTS)
F166   8D 15                  BSR   hexc   
F168   24 08                  BCC   hx2   ; odd digit: already consumed, dropped
F16A   AA 84                  ORA   ,X   
F16C   A7 80                  STA   ,X+   
F16E   8D 0D                  BSR   hexc   
F170   25 EF                  BCS   hx1   
F172   86 39        HX2:      LDA   #39h   ; RTS
F174   A7 84                  STA   ,X   
F176   AF C4                  STX   ,U   
F178   39                     RTS      
F179   AE C1        EXEC:     LDX   ,U++   
F17B   6E 84                  JMP   ,X   ; the code's RTS returns to the dispatcher
F17D                          IF   CHECKED   
F17D   BD F0 B4     HEXC:     JSR   isdig   
F180   25 0A                  BLO   hv1   ; C = 1
F182   84 DF                  ANDA   #0DFh   
F184   80 11                  SUBA   #11h   
F186   81 06                  CMPA   #6   
F188   24 04                  BHS   hv9   ; C = 0
F18A   80 F6                  SUBA   #0F6h   ; + 10, and sets the carry
F18C   31 21        HV1:      LEAY   1,Y   ; (LEAY leaves the carry alone)
F18E   39           HV9:      RTS      
F18F                          ENDIF      
F18F                          IF   1-CHECKED   
                    GETK:     EQU   KEY   ; the host's routine does the whole job
F18F                          ENDIF      
F18F                             ; ---- where the two ranges meet ---------------------------------------------
F18F                             ; The first range ends somewhere among the next five handlers, ] \ [ @ ?
F18F                             ; (they are in falling code order for this): NPG1 follows. Every handler
F18F                             ; below them must start in the second range, and every one above in the
F18F                             ; first.
F18F                             ; "]" returns: pop an address and jump to it. (PULS X,Y: the dispatcher's
F18F                             ; return address into X, then the saved IP into Y.)
F18F                H_RET:       
F18F                          IF   CHECKED   
F18F   BD F3 07               JSR   chkbrk   ; Ctrl-C is checked at every word return
F192                          ENDIF      
F192   35 30                  PULS   X,Y   
F194   6E 84                  JMP   ,X   
